## Phase 1 — StudentProfileSnapshot (do this first, ship it alone)

Create `server/models/StudentProfileSnapshot.js`:
- `user` (ObjectId ref User, indexed), `dateKey` (YYYY-MM-DD string)
- the 12 score fields above (numeric ones as Number, the string/array ones as-is)
- a small `signals` sub-object of raw counters worth trending: streak,
  consistency, pendingTasks, overdueTasks, applicationsCount, resumeCompletion,
  stressLevel, studyMinutes — pull whatever the collectors already return; do
  not invent new collection logic
- `contextQualityScore` is already in the scores; also store `collectedAt`
- Unique compound index on `{ user: 1, dateKey: 1 }` so re-runs are idempotent
- Index `{ user: 1, dateKey: -1 }` for trend queries

Create `server/automation/intelligence/snapshotProfiles.js`:
- Export `snapshotProfiles()`, wrapped in `runJob('profile-snapshot', ...)`
- Iterate **active** users only — define active as having any activity in the
  last N days (check what signal is cheapest and most reliable; `ActivityLog`,
  `User.lastLoginAt`, or recent `ChatMessage`/`Task` writes — pick one and
  document the choice). Do NOT load all users into memory: batch with a cursor
  or paginate, and cap concurrency (e.g. 5 at a time) so 9 collector queries ×
  N users doesn't melt Atlas.
- For each user call `buildStudentProfile(userId)` and upsert the snapshot on
  `{ user, dateKey }`.
- Skip users whose `contextQualityScore` is 0 (no data — a snapshot of nothing
  is noise).
- Log counts processed/skipped/failed; never let one user's failure abort the run.

Register it at ~02:30 UTC (08:00 IST) as `CRON_PROFILE_SNAPSHOT`, before the
briefing job at 06:00.

Then add `server/ai/intelligence-layer/trends.js`:
- `getTrend(userId, metric, { days = 30 })` → the series
- `getDelta(userId, metric, { days = 14 })` → `{ start, end, delta, pctChange }`
- `summarizeTrends(userId)` → a short human-readable string of only the
  *notable* movements (e.g. changes beyond a threshold), suitable for injection
  into a prompt. Keep it terse — this goes into a token budget.

**Acceptance:** running the job twice on the same day creates exactly one
snapshot per user. Tests cover idempotency, the active-user filter, the
skip-empty rule, and `getDelta` maths.

---

## Phase 2 — Persist the learning loop

Move `learningLoop.js`'s in-memory `_overrides` Map into MongoDB
(`server/models/GeneratorWeight.js` or a field on an existing per-user prefs
model — check `UserModelPref.js` first and reuse if it fits).

- Keep the existing public API (`getGeneratorWeight`, `processFeedback`) so
  callers in `recommendation-engine/index.js` don't change.
- `getGeneratorWeight` is called in hot paths, so read through a small in-process
  cache with a TTL rather than hitting Mongo per call; write through on update.
- Preserve the existing MIN_WEIGHT/MAX_WEIGHT clamps.

**Acceptance:** learned weights survive a simulated process restart in a test.

---

## Phase 3 — Prediction ledger (the differentiator — do not skip)

The goal: when Dax makes a forward-looking claim, it is recorded, later
resolved against reality, and Dax's own accuracy is shown to the student.

Create `server/models/DaxPrediction.js`:
- `user`, `statement` (the human sentence Dax asserted), `metric` (which score
  it's about, e.g. `careerReadiness`), `predictedValue`, `comparator`
  (`gte`/`lte`/`eq`), `horizonDays`, `predictedAt`, `resolveBy` (Date, indexed),
  `resolvedAt`, `actualValue`, `outcome` (enum:
  `pending|hit|miss|partial|unresolvable`), `sourceTask`, `model`, `provider`
- Index `{ outcome: 1, resolveBy: 1 }` for the resolver, `{ user: 1, predictedAt: -1 }`
  for display.

Recording: start narrow and deterministic. Do **not** try to parse arbitrary
free-form chat for predictions. Instead, record predictions from the structured
places where Dax already produces forward-looking output — start with the
recommendation engine's readiness/goal-progress paths
(`recommendation-engine/goalProgress.js`, `weeklyReview.js`) and add an explicit
`recordPrediction()` call there. Note in comments that chat-sourced predictions
are a later phase.

Resolver: `server/automation/intelligence/resolvePredictions.js`, daily.
For each `pending` prediction whose `resolveBy` has passed, look up the
`StudentProfileSnapshot` nearest that date, compare, set outcome. If no snapshot
exists in a reasonable window, mark `unresolvable` — never guess.

Expose `getAccuracy(userId)` → `{ total, hits, misses, accuracy }` plus the
recent resolved list, and a read-only route for the client.

**This must be honest.** Show misses as prominently as hits. An assistant that
says "I predicted 5 weeks, it took 8" is the entire point of the feature. Do not
add logic that hides or softens misses.

**Acceptance:** tests cover hit, miss, and unresolvable paths, and confirm the
resolver is idempotent (re-running doesn't double-resolve).

---

## Phase 4 — Cohort insights (privacy-gated)

Let Dax say things like "students in your batch who converted this drive had 3×
more STAR stories by this point" — something a general chatbot structurally
cannot say.

- Build aggregates over cohorts defined by fields that already exist on
  `StudentIdentity` / `UserProfile` / `ProgramRegistry` (batch, college,
  program) joined with `PlacementOutcome`.
- **Hard privacy rules, non-negotiable:**
  - Suppress any cohort with fewer than 5 members (k-anonymity). Return null,
    not a smaller sample.
  - Return aggregates only — never individual students, names, or anything
    re-identifiable.
  - Respect existing visibility settings; read `server/models/profileVisibility.js`
    and extend that model rather than inventing a parallel one.
  - Never expose another student's raw data through Dax's context.
- Precompute nightly into a cached collection; do not aggregate per request.

**Acceptance:** a test proves a 4-member cohort returns nothing and a 5-member
cohort returns aggregates only.

---

## Phase 5 — Make the crons push judgment, not just content

The existing ~20 jobs push content ("here's today's case"). Add one that pushes
judgment: e.g. a student 6 days from a placement drive with 3 overdue tasks and
a falling consistency trend gets a specific, actionable nudge with an offer to
replan — using the Phase 1 trend data.

Reuse the existing notification path (`controllers/notificationController.js`,
`notifyBulk`) and the existing quiet-hours/rate-limit conventions if any exist —
check before adding new ones. Cap frequency hard (at most one judgment nudge per
student per day, and only when a real threshold is crossed). A nudge that fires
every day is spam and will get Dax muted.

---

## Phase 6 — Actually use it (otherwise none of the above matters)

Wire the new state into Dax's context so it changes what Dax says:
- Inject `summarizeTrends(userId)` output into the enriched context built by
  `intelligence-layer/profileFactory.js` → `buildEnrichedContext()`, so the
  chat path sees trajectory, not just current state.
- Keep it token-cheap: only notable deltas, capped length.
- Extend `server/ai/dax.js`'s guidance so Dax cites the evidence when it makes a
  claim about the student ("your consistency is down 30% since the 10th"),
  and never fabricates a trend when no snapshot history exists yet.
- Add a test in the style of `server/tests/daxSystemPrompt.test.js`.

